WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
Mobile app testing spans a wide spectrum.

2
00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:07.520
Unit tests on the left verify single components using Mox.

3
00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:09.760
Integration tests sit in the middle.

4
00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.640
Here, we render the real-screen component with its actual children,

5
00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:17.840
intercepting only external systems outside the JavaScript boundary.

6
00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:19.880
If you come from an iOS background,

7
00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:22.720
this architectural philosophy translates directly.

8
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:28.240
An XC test integration suite typically spins up a real UI view controller with its subviews.

9
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.840
The goal is to assert that tapping a button triggers the intended UI state transition

10
00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:34.840
across the whole view hierarchy.

11
00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:40.160
In React Native, the UI view controller concept maps directly to the React test renderer.

12
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.320
Instead of a native view, it builds a virtual component tree purely on the JavaScript side.

13
00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:48.760
You don't need to test the actual native navigation framework itself.

14
00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:53.800
You simply need to verify that your screen triggers the correct transition intent when users act.

15
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:56.840
This setup is optimized for complex data flows.

16
00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:00.400
Verifying user input, checking form validation,

17
00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:05.920
and the resulting screen updates is the primary scenario this testing tier was built for.

18
00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:10.280
By mastering this boundary, you gain the execution speed of a unit test

19
00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:16.000
while maintaining the application level coverage typically associated with heavy end-to-end tests.

20
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.320
To achieve that coverage without flakiness,

21
00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:22.800
we have to construct the first layer of isolation, the network boundary.

22
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:24.840
We use Mox service worker.

23
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:32.080
In React Native, MSW intercepts HTTP requests at the node level by patching global fetch APIs.

24
00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:37.800
Since React Native lacks browser service workers, we rely on direct node patching instead.

25
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:43.000
This operates just like URL protocol subclassing in native iOS development.

26
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.360
You register a handler that intercepts requests matching a specific pattern.

27
00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:51.240
This allows your tests to return precisely crafted data responses

28
00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:54.200
without touching the production code that makes the request.

29
00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.680
But to guarantee test isolation, we have to configure the MSW server

30
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.280
with one specific restrictive setting.

31
00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:04.760
You must set the on-handled request property to error.

32
00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:10.200
If you emit this, any unmarked request your test makes will execute against a real end point,

33
00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:15.000
breaking determinism and leading directly to flaky continuous integration pipelines.

34
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:19.400
Enforcing strict error-throwing network interception ensures the test environment

35
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.960
has absolute authority over all external data inputs.

36
00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:27.160
Once the network is controlled, we have to lock down the internal state layer.

37
00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:31.160
TANSTAC query is the next major source of unpredictable tests.

38
00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:36.400
By design, TANSTAC query caches results and automatically retries failed requests.

39
00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.320
In production, this is a feature.

40
00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.120
Across test timelines, it breaks isolation.

41
00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.240
If you construct a single query client at the module level,

42
00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:47.680
cache data from a successful test will bleed into the next test,

43
00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.880
falsely satisfying its requirements.

44
00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:53.280
The second risk comes from the default retry logic,

45
00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:57.120
which uses exponential back-off when a network call fails.

46
00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.560
When you write an integration test specifically to verify an error state,

47
00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.160
this default behavior forces the test to wait several seconds for retries,

48
00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.120
often triggering timeouts.

49
00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:11.480
The architectural solution is to build a factory wrapper

50
00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:16.000
that generates a fresh, isolated query client for every single test.

51
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.320
Configure this factory with three parameters

52
00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:20.600
to build a firewall between tests.

53
00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:23.960
Setting retry to zero instantly surfaces errors.

54
00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:28.280
Setting GC try to zero forces immediate garbage collection of the cache.

55
00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:33.440
Finally, a zero-stale time guarantees every mount triggers a fresh network fetch.

56
00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:38.240
When you combine this factory with a call to server.reset handlers after each execution,

57
00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.400
you sanitize the entire environment.

58
00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:43.480
This hermetic state isolation prevents test pollution,

59
00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:48.720
where a single failure or success is determined by the specific order of the test suite execution.

60
00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:52.520
With state and network isolated, the final architectural layer

61
00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:57.160
is synchronizing our simulated user actions with the JavaScript event loop.

62
00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:01.080
There was a significant change in react native testing library version 12

63
00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.560
regarding how we interact with the UI.

64
00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:07.600
All user event methods like pressing a button or typing text

65
00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.920
are now asynchronous operations.

66
00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:12.680
If you forget the await keyword before these actions,

67
00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:14.560
you create a race condition.

68
00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:20.240
The test runner will execute your assertions before the simulated interaction has actually been processed.

69
00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:24.280
The same timing rules apply when querying data after an interaction.

70
00:04:24.280 --> 00:04:29.960
The network responses we configured in NSW only resolve on the next tick of the event loop.

71
00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:33.640
Because of this, the first assertion you make that checks for fetch data

72
00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:35.960
must be wrapped in a wait for block.

73
00:04:35.960 --> 00:04:40.240
Mastering these async boundaries is how you accurately model the temporal reality

74
00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:43.520
of a user interacting with network dependent interfaces.

75
00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:49.200
Let's review the architecture we've built and compare it directly back to the native iOS standard.

76
00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.240
First, the render environment.

77
00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:55.120
XTest uses a real UI kit hierarchy on a simulator.

78
00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:58.040
React native uses a virtual component tree.

79
00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:01.360
For networks, native subclasses URL protocol,

80
00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:04.280
whereas we patch fetch using MSW.

81
00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:07.200
Finally, iOS manages animation timing

82
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.720
while React native handles async updates and retries.

83
00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:15.040
The underlying goal of testing state transitions is identical in both worlds,

84
00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:18.320
even if the physical execution mechanisms are decoupled.

85
00:05:18.320 --> 00:05:21.360
By operating entirely within the JavaScript layer,

86
00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:24.360
we can run hundreds of integration tests in seconds,

87
00:05:24.360 --> 00:05:27.600
bypassing the overhead of booting an iOS simulator.

88
00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:31.400
However, this architecture cannot catch native rendering bugs.

89
00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:35.360
Issues tied to core animation or layout errors in the fabric renderer

90
00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:39.280
will pass in the JavaScript layer, but fail on a physical device.

91
00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:42.080
This is where detox end-to-end tests come in.

92
00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:47.120
They should be reserved for verifying these specific native layout integrations,

93
00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:49.920
rather than complex business logic.

94
00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:54.280
Combining a JavaScript integration layer with targeted detox tests

95
00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:57.520
allows teams to verify business logic at scale,

96
00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:00.160
while catching native regressions on the device.

